From 854e58b5f90b8eae5735b1c96ff67d57849109b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sasha Zero <sasha@ripostelabs.xyz>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 14:33:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Add HEVC mvd hooks to ffedit by CABAC re-encoding

Export and re-apply every coded mvd of P and B prediction units as
the "mv" (final vector, mvp + mvd) and "mv_delta" (raw mvd) features.

HEVC slice data is CABAC only, so nothing can be rewritten in place:
the arithmetic coder state after an edited value differs and every
following bit changes. Instead every bin the decoder reads is logged
with its context (or bypass/terminate), the bins of an edited mvd
group are replaced by the binarisation of the new value, and the
slice is re-encoded from the logged bins with a CABAC encoder started
from the slice's initial context states. Context selection never
depends on an mvd value and the group is contiguous, so nothing else
moves. The slice header is copied, rbsp_slice_segment_trailing_bits
are regenerated and emulation prevention is re-inserted. NALs without
edits are copied raw.

Applying the exported JSON back onto an edited stream reproduces the
x265 original byte for byte (tested on 20 encoder configurations,
Main and Main 10, CTB 16/32/64, B-pyramid, AMP, weighted prediction,
lossless CUs, transform skip, 1280x720). Refused with a clear message
when an mv feature is requested: WPP, tiles, dependent slice segments,
PCM, CABAC bypass alignment, hvcC input, more than one thread.

MAX_MV2DARRAY_NBLOCKS grows to 128: a 64x64 CTB holds up to 128
inter PUs per list.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
